6. Why use MediaPipe instead of Haar Cascade?

"Haar Cascade only detects the face or eyes as rectangular regions. MediaPipe provides precise facial landmarks with hundreds of key points, allowing accurate eye state estimation."

7. Why use OpenCV?

"OpenCV handles webcam access, image capture, frame processing, and basic computer vision operations before the frames are analysed by MediaPipe."

8. Why use PyInstaller?

"PyInstaller packages the Python application along with its dependencies into a standalone executable, allowing users to run it without installing Python."

9. Why use Inno Setup?

"Inno Setup creates a professional Windows installer. It copies the application into Program Files, creates desktop and Start Menu shortcuts, and registers uninstall information, making installation similar to commercial Windows applications."

10. Complete Flow (Best Answer)

If an interviewer asks "How does your project work?", you can say:

"The user launches the application and clicks Activate. The GUI starts a background detection thread so the interface remains responsive. The thread captures webcam frames using OpenCV and sends each frame to MediaPipe Face Landmarker. MediaPipe detects facial landmarks and returns landmark coordinates. My application extracts only the eye landmarks and computes the Eye Aspect Ratio to determine whether the eyes are open or closed. If the eyes remain closed continuously beyond the configured threshold, the application classifies it as drowsiness and plays an alarm using pygame. The application runs in the Windows system tray, allowing monitoring in the background. Finally, I packaged it as a standalone executable with PyInstaller and distributed it through an installer built using Inno Setup."
